          ],
          "text": "According to BNSF Railway (Petitioner), according to BNSF Railway (Petitioner), bNSF contends that the Montana Supreme Court\u2019s conclusions about general personal jurisdiction are wrong because they rely on systematic activity and Section 56 of the Federal Employers' Liability Act."
        },
        {
          "attribution": "Ms. Murray",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "86c8545503432c2bb2f43fc8fe373ae3723e99d4b40cc1a68924adba0a1434ce",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-86c854550343-0002-0001"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 39,
                "start": 39
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to Ms. Murray, bNSF argues that its revenue share in Montana (up to ten percent) is disproportionately large, showing a substantial business presence in the state."
        },
        {
          "attribution": "BNSF",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "86c8545503432c2bb2f43fc8fe373ae3723e99d4b40cc1a68924adba0a1434ce",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-86c854550343-0001-0003"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 27,
                "start": 27
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to BNSF, bNSF relies on the Pope and Boyd cases, which support the view that a railroad\u2019s business activities in a state can create jurisdiction for suits filed elsewhere."
        }
